</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_Oxtoby_et_al./14E:_Chemical_Equilibrium_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_34"><span id="Q73"></span><h2 id="Q73-49234"><span>Q73 </span></h2>

<p><span>At 2</span>5&deg;C<span>, 0.130 g/L of chloroacetic acid (</span><span>C</span><sub>2</sub><span>H</span><sub>3</sub><span>ClO</span><sub>2</sub><span>) dissolve in of water, and at the same temperature 0.2834</span><span> g/L of chloroacetic acid dissolve in 1-octanol. </span></p>

<p><span>a. Calculate the following equilibrium constants </span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[\ce{C2H2ClO2H(s)} \rightleftharpoons \ce{C2H2ClO2H}_{(aq)} \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[\ce{C2H2ClO2H(s)} \rightleftharpoons \ce{C2H2ClO2H}_{(octanol)} \nonumber \]</p>

<p><span>b. Calculate the <em>participation coefficient</em>, \(K\), of the combined reactions.</span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[\ce{C2H2ClO2H(aq)} \rightleftharpoons \ce{C2H2ClO2H}_{(octanol)} \nonumber \]</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Solution</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p>First, we will need g/L to M so that we can calculate the equilibrium constant.</p>

    <p class="mt-align-center">\[\left(\dfrac{0.130\; \cancel{g\; C&shy;_{2}H_{3}ClO_{2}} }{1\;L}\right) \left(\dfrac{1\: mol}{94.494\: \cancel{ g\; C&shy;_{2}H_{3}ClO_{2}}} \right) = 0.0013757\; M\nonumber \]</p>

    <p class="mt-align-center">\[\left(\dfrac{0.2834\; \cancel{g\; C&shy;_{2}H_{3}ClO_{2}} }{1\;L}\right) \left(\dfrac{1\: mol}{94.494\: \cancel{ g\; C&shy;_{2}H_{3}ClO_{2}}} \right) = 3.00x10^{-3}\; M\nonumber \]</p>

    <p>\[K = \dfrac{K_{forminde}}{K_{aq}}=\dfrac{3.00 \times 10^{-3}}{0.001376} =2.18\nonumber \]</p>

    <p class="mt-align-center">\[\log_{10} K_{oct/wat} =0.338\nonumber \]</p>
    </dd>
</dl>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_Oxtoby_et_al./14E:_Chemical_Equilibrium_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_35"><span id="Q75"></span><h2 id="Q75-49234">Q75</h2>

<p><span>At 298 K, the two gases NOBr and Br<sub>2 </sub>have the number of moles of 1.25 and 8.5 x 10<sup>-5</sup> at equilibrium with a sufficient amount of solid NO. Determine the equilibrium constant of the reaction: </span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[2NOBr_{(g)} \rightleftharpoons 2NO_{(s)} + Br_{2(g)} \nonumber \]</p>

